From f8f8fe9211209b0ac888f7c6b37dcedb5fa08824 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:35:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(movement): send completed local physics to ViaBedrock

Send the local player's position, predicted motion and swimming
state after sendChanges and before Java CLIENT_TICK_END. Use the
negotiated, versioned core channel on direct connections and ViaProxy.
A stationary player still sends a fresh sample, so fluid gravity and pose
transitions do not depend on Java's sparse position updates.

Remove the Entity-wide swimming hook, which could turn another entity's
pose change into local auth input and required an in-process core tracker.
The core now validates and consumes local frames and emits swimming edges.
This integrates the existing Bedrock fluid physics with auth-input packet
construction without changing server corrections or vehicle prediction.

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 captures report -0.022315647 fluid motion
where the old core's air approximation reports -0.0996117 at the same
water position. Java 26.3 bytecode confirms the sampled method runs after
local physics and standard movement, before the tick-end packet.

Native sprint swimming starts on the first movement frame. Java updates
swimming before aiStep computes sprinting, delaying its transition by one
frame. Refresh the local swimming state after sprint changes and before
fluid travel on Bedrock connections. Keep ordinary Java timing intact.

Run the local swimming update after sprint decisions and before fluid travel.
Native 1.26.51.1 wall and open-water captures stop swimming on forward release
while retaining sprint drag until the next frame. Preserve that physics
phase directly instead of delaying protocol events independently. Include
completed collision axes so stationary frames cannot retain stale flags.

Keep the fluid inset symmetric for short swimming boxes. Java normalizes
inverted AABBs, which moved the water boundary below the box center and
delayed native surface exits. Avoid the stale visual crawling slowdown
on that exit frame and retain sprinting in shallow water. Native 1.26.51.1
protocol 2193 surface captures establish these transitions.

Inspect the matching native SwimTriggerSystem and PlayerBoundingBoxStateUpdateSystem
to reproduce upward swimming re-entry. Native checks the breathing-point
material and the block above the single-precision collision-box center when
look Y is at least 0.15. Gate the local transition with those probes instead
of fitting an eye height or frame delay to the trajectory. Preserve the
previous swimming state for the verified sprint-release phase.

Stop upward swimming when the native breathing probe reaches air and
standing is unobstructed. Reproduce the trigger's acos of the squared
horizontal direction components instead of using the body-water predicate.
The first revised strict-BDS run exposed that difference on the second
surface exit after re-entry. The completed direct comparison now matches
the native pose and sprint events at all three reference angles without
corrections during the controlled swimming cases.

Use the matching native SwimControlSystem's breathing-material condition
for non-jumping, non-flying upward swimming. Clear vertical motion whenever
the breathing point is outside liquid, rather than applying a fitted
look-Y cutoff of 0.55. Preserve swimming when the standing-space probe
is blocked, as the native swimming trigger does.

Strict-BDS ceiling tests expose an additional sprint and fluid-state
transition gap. The revised path settles at the server's capped surface
after two direct corrections or three ViaProxy corrections instead of
repeatedly changing pose and receiving 22 direct corrections. Keep that
remaining transition gap explicit; the native open-water comparison still
covers separate unobstructed cases.
